# `Tcaches`'s byte offsets and layout, which the build checks with `fits`.
T_TABLE	core library	Kani	discharged (const assert: `axiom_tcaches_layout` in src/heap/tcaches.rs)	contract 2097268554, body none
T_LOCK	core library	Kani	discharged (const assert: `axiom_tcaches_layout` in src/heap/tcaches.rs)	contract 2489631139, body none
T_INV	core library	Kani	discharged (const assert: `axiom_tcaches_layout` in src/heap/tcaches.rs)	contract 1448314863, body none
axiom_tcaches_layout	core library	Kani	discharged (const assert: `axiom_tcaches_layout` in src/heap/tcaches.rs)	contract 121335155, body none, spec 3940336727
# Joining typed field permissions into the struct's: no vstd counterpart.
# The harness runs `Tcaches::init`, the one call, which
# `crate::tests::assembled_writes_only_at_checked_sites` pins, and checks
# that its writes leave every field byte of the `Tcaches` as the axiom's
# `ensures` state.
axiom_tcaches_assemble	raw pointer (internal)	Kani	trusted (partly checked, Kani: heap::tcaches::kani_assemble::init_writes_every_field; not checked: the join itself, that the field permissions and `pad` may be exchanged for one `PointsTo<Tcaches>` at `p`, with its provenance, a premise of Verus's own model that no tool checks; the ghost identities the `ensures` carries over, each table entry's atomic identity (`vspec::entry::entry_id`, which the `inv`'s key records) and the lock's cell identity, which Kani does not observe; and the ghost fields (`inv`, the lock's rank and initial value), which have no bytes)	contract 2409023419, body none, spec 3892657697
# The C caller uses an explicit cache on one thread at a time, and a
# heap's key destructor runs once: each call that reaches an existing
# heap through `heap_at` makes one token, before any reference it lends.
axiom_heap_use	C-program contract	none (assumption)
# A raw-pointer dereference: a live heap from creation is well formed,
# has its home headers at `p` and keeps the rest of its block with the
# block's claim (`Heap::head`). The reference borrows the caller's
# `HeapUse` token, so one token gives one reference at a time; two
# tokens rest on `axiom_heap_use`'s premise.
heap_at	allocator invariant	none (assumption)
